[Music]  hello and welcome to the session on  DevOps workflow in this session we will  understand what a workflow is how it is  applicable for a particular software  development project and what are the  various tools that can be used to create  the workflow workflow allows you the  ability to create a complex set of  repeatable tasks it's like a sticky  wrapper in the DevOps continuous  delivery that holds it all together for  you workflows work as a glue between  various processes and systems while  allowing you to easily track their  progress  imagine having perfect test automation  scripts but no easy way to execute them  outside of constructing a new build or  having a perfect CMDB integration script  but no way to call them outside of the  build process or even worse having a  need for human approval before a  deployment takes place and no way to  integrate it outside of a custom  deployable process our usual software  development cycle looks something like  this  developers need a pre-configured  environment with all the latest services  running that is consistent and the same  for each of the developers on the whole  team developers check in their co-ed in  the git repository from where it picked  up and the build is run you deploy the  latest release of the software  application to a temporary server deploy  your test harness to test your software  application and after all tests are  complete just get rid of that server and  environment set up your production  environment and deploy the latest  release of your software application to  prod accordingly send off notifications  at each of the steps noting the  completion status success and failure  these are the tasks we do in our  day-to-day in our project work  operations this is what we call a  typical SDLC project cycle with a few  variations here and there  when you are designing a workflow try to  break down automation constructs into  logical functions that are intended to  provide build automation for example you  should not include turning monitor  functions on or off something like a  build automation also should not have to  worry about the deployment process its  primary singular purpose is to construct  a new build where in that new build is  then deployed and becomes a single  purpose of the deployment process  attempting to automate the entire cycle  of an application into one automation  construct from end to end instead of  having small blocks that work together  increases the size of it which increases  the risk and increases the runtime which  makes it difficult to debug if there is  any problem also doing so takes away the  ability to run any one of these steps if  you want to rerun them at any point when  you are planning for a workflow a broad  overview can get you started on  assembling your workflow from the top  down the most important point when it  comes to planning your flow is that you  actually do it making a good plan before  you start a workflow will end up saving  a lot of time and heartache in the long  run when you are planning a workflow  drawing out the flow to help you  visualize the flow logic or writing out  a sketch in some textual form like  swimlane concepts can be great ways to  help identify what systems are involved  and what it's doing  using placeholder steps as you gradually  build up your flows interconnecting  parts let's take a moment to talk about  human intervention in a typical workflow  that we follow for software development  life cycles every time you are ready to  do a build you have to seek approvals  you have to seek sign offs you have to  wait for the people to be available to  come to a meeting to come to checkpoints  if you have automated your workflow and  every time you have to pause your  automation to obtain a human approval  you are unnecessarily introducing time  delays that cannot be predicted or occur  with any kind of consistency  similarly imagine a deployment process  that required a human to press yes  before continuing the introduction of a  human approval can sit idle on a tooling  screen for hours during lunch or any  unexpected days off or the person being  on vacation automate processes with such  quality and consistency so that the  human intervention could merely become  redundant and unnecessary workflow  rapper is a highly functional tool to  allow you to redesign more effective  automation processes limiting human  interaction where possible again the  goal is to build small independent  automation constructs that work together  in a highly efficient manner when you  sit down to create a workflow keep a  rule of thumb in mind that a big  workflow is a bad workflow big flows are  hard to create hard to read hard to test  hard to maintain and even hard to use  when you are creating a workflow break  down the large flow into smaller more  manageable units that can flow  themselves each unit can have a single  well focused purpose if you narrow down  the scope of each of the flows  components you basically make each  component easier to test and easier to  fix when something goes wrong or perhaps  needs updating smaller less complex  units are easier to test independently  and every time you have an iterative  task ask yourself if this can be done in  parallel instead of basically doing the  same work and in lesser time if you have  smaller chunks of manageable work then  you can actually make them run in  parallel rather than waiting for the  sequence to run when you are creating  workflows consider your error handling  minimize errors and make your debugging  as easy as possible start your flow with  an initialization step that would  basically sanitize all parameters before  they are being used in your workflow  handle all possible null values that  could possibly arise results like  success and failure are simple and easy  to understand use a more robust set of  rules that will impart a bit more  information as to what exactly what  when you are creating a workflow take  care of the notifications notifications  can also be handled in workflow wrapper  much better than an individual DevOps  tools for example a release manager  wanted to know the build processes for  his or her application would have to  build a notification method embedded  within build processes in order to know  if the build passed or failed an  alternative is the workflow wrapper just  check the build logs and you can send  notifications in the preferred method  whether it's email a text message or  even an Instagram picture additionally  one may not check the status of each and  every build created they may want to  know only when a build gets passed and  is ready for further testing  environments even departmental  notification escalation chains can be  incorporated easily in a workflow  wrapper and executes as expected but not  within each DevOps tool when you are  creating a workflow definitely consider  release management your release  orchestration tools have some minimal  workflow capabilities built into their  offerings most of the release management  tooling is designed to put an elegant  wrapper around the build automation and  deployment processes and it has the  ability to segment by line of business  or product as well as role based access  controls for permissions and edit  auditory complaints what release  management excels in our human  interactions and entire tracking  progress is of your release deployment  highly evolved notification systems tend  to require an external workflow wrapper  beyond the normal capabilities that a  release management tool can offer a  solid workflow wrapper acts as a glue  that can hold desperate DevOps tools  together  hey once become an expert in cloud  computing then subscribe to simpler  Channel and click here to watch more  such videos turn it up and get certified  in cloud computing click here

